  "response_complete": true,
  "response_text": "When preparing to disclose a disability at work, it's important to document incidents that may be relevant to your disability. Here are some steps you can take to document incidents:\n\n1. Keep a journal: Keep a journal of your experiences and any incidents that may be relevant to your disability. This can include details about your symptoms, how they affect your daily life, and any accommodations you have requested or received.\n\n2. Document medical records: Keep copies of your medical records, including any diagnoses, treatments, and medications. This can be helpful if you need to provide documentation to your employer.\n\n3. Document accommodations: Keep a record of any accommodations you have requested or received from your employer. This can include details about the accommodations, how they were implemented, and any issues that arose.\n\n4. Document any discrimination: If you believe you have been discriminated against because of your disability, keep a record of any incidents that occurred. This can include details about the incidents, how they affected you, and any steps you took to address them.\n\n5. Seek legal advice: If you are unsure about how to document incidents or if you believe you have been discriminated against, consider seeking legal advice. A lawyer can help you understand your rights and provide guidance on how to document incidents and address any discrimination.\n\nRemember, it's important to document incidents in a clear and concise manner. This can help you provide a comprehensive and accurate account of your disability and any incidents that may be relevant to your disability.",
